From: MacSW maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: Read the own module list locally (MACSW_LOCAL_MODULE_READS=1).

EnumProcessModules(), GetModuleBaseNameW(), GetModuleFileNameExW() and
GetModuleInformation() walk the loader list of the target process with one
ReadProcessMemory() per entry. NtReadVirtualMemory() copies locally only for
the GetCurrentProcess() pseudo handle; a real handle to the own process
(DuplicateHandle(GetCurrentProcess()), OpenProcess(GetCurrentProcessId()))
makes one wineserver round trip per entry. SOLIDWORKS 2026 lists the modules
of its own process this way while it starts (about 400 modules, one walk per
module and call): 129,572 read_process_memory requests on its main thread
before the main window.

With MACSW_LOCAL_MODULE_READS=1, init_module_iterator() uses the pseudo handle
when the process id of the handle is our own, so the walk reads local memory
(NtReadVirtualMemory's existing __TRY/memmove path). The first read of each
walk (LdrData from the PEB) still goes through the caller's handle, so the
server checks PROCESS_VM_READ as before: a handle with only
PROCESS_QUERY_(LIMITED_)INFORMATION still fails with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED.
The results are the same; other processes still go through the server.
Without the variable nothing changes. MacSW sets it for SOLIDWORKS and
sw-cli launches.

diff --git a/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c b/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
index 8dbb677..01d7fe2 100644
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
@@ -942,6 +942,25 @@ static BOOL init_module_iterator_wow64( struct module_iterator *iter, HANDLE pro
 }
 
 
+/* MacSW: with MACSW_LOCAL_MODULE_READS=1, walk the module list of our own process through the
+ * current-process pseudo handle when the caller passed a real handle to it (DuplicateHandle or
+ * OpenProcess of our own pid). ReadProcessMemory then copies locally instead of making one
+ * wineserver round trip per list entry; the psapi functions below walk the list once per call.
+ * The first read of a walk still goes through the caller's handle, so a handle without
+ * PROCESS_VM_READ fails with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED as in stock Wine. */
+static BOOL local_module_reads(void)
+{
+    static LONG enabled = -1;
+
+    if (enabled == -1)
+    {
+        WCHAR value[4];
+        DWORD len = GetEnvironmentVariableW( L"MACSW_LOCAL_MODULE_READS", value, ARRAY_SIZE(value) );
+        InterlockedExchange( &enabled, len == 1 && value[0] == '1' );
+    }
+    return enabled;
+}
+
 static BOOL init_module_iterator( struct module_iterator *iter, HANDLE process )
 {
     PROCESS_BASIC_INFORMATION pbi;
@@ -956,6 +975,12 @@ static BOOL init_module_iterator( struct module_iterator *iter, HANDLE process )
     if (!ReadProcessMemory( process, &pbi.PebBaseAddress->LdrData, &ldr_data, sizeof(ldr_data), NULL ))
         return FALSE;
 
+    /* MacSW: this first read went through the caller's handle, so the server has checked
+     * PROCESS_VM_READ as before; the rest of the walk of our own list is read locally. */
+    if (process != GetCurrentProcess() && pbi.UniqueProcessId == GetCurrentProcessId()
+        && local_module_reads())
+        process = GetCurrentProcess();
+
     /* This happens when running "old" wow64 configuration. Mark it as such. */
     if (!ldr_data)
     {
